@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "SQL Command Categories";
}

<section>

    <!-- PAGE INTRODUCTION -->
    <div class="mb-5">

        <h1 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            SQL Command Categories
        </h1>

        <p class="lead text-muted">
            SQL commands are divided into different categories based on
            the type of operation they perform. Understanding these
            categories makes it easier to identify which SQL command
            should be used for a particular database operation.
        </p>

        <p>
            In SQL Server, commands are commonly studied under five major
            categories: <strong>DDL, DML, DQL, DCL and TCL</strong>.
            Each category has a different responsibility when working
            with databases and data.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- OVERVIEW -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            SQL Command Categories Overview
        </h2>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Category</th>
                        <th>Full Form</th>
                        <th>Main Purpose</th>
                        <th>Common Commands</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>

                        <td>
                            <strong>DDL</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Data Definition Language
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Defines and changes the structure of
                            database objects.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            CREATE, ALTER, DROP, TRUNCATE
                        </td>

                    </tr>

                    <tr>

                        <td>
                            <strong>DML</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Data Manipulation Language
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Adds, modifies and removes data.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE
                        </td>

                    </tr>

                    <tr>

                        <td>
                            <strong>DQL</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Data Query Language
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Retrieves data from tables.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            SELECT
                        </td>

                    </tr>

                    <tr>

                        <td>
                            <strong>DCL</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Data Control Language
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Controls permissions and access.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            GRANT, REVOKE
                        </td>

                    </tr>

                    <tr>

                        <td>
                            <strong>TCL</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Transaction Control Language
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Manages transactions.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            COMMIT, ROLLBACK
                        </td>

                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- DDL -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            1. DDL – Data Definition Language
        </h2>

        <p>
            DDL stands for <strong>Data Definition Language</strong>.
            DDL commands are mainly used to define and modify the
            structure of database objects.
        </p>

        <p>
            Database objects include databases, tables, views and other
            objects. DDL commands do not primarily deal with changing
            individual records; they work with the structure of objects.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Common DDL Commands
        </h5>

        <ul>

            <li class="mb-2">
                <strong>CREATE</strong> – Creates a database object.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                <strong>ALTER</strong> – Changes the structure of an
                existing object.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                <strong>DROP</strong> – Removes a database object.
            </li>

            <li>
                <strong>TRUNCATE</strong> – Removes all rows from a table
                while keeping the table structure.
            </li>

        </ul>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            CREATE Example
        </h5>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>CREATE TABLE Students
(
    StudentId INT,
    StudentName VARCHAR(100),
    Age INT
);</code></pre>

        </div>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            ALTER Example
        </h5>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>ALTER TABLE Students
ADD Email VARCHAR(100);</code></pre>

        </div>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            DROP Example
        </h5>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>DROP TABLE Students;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- DML -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            2. DML – Data Manipulation Language
        </h2>

        <p>
            DML stands for <strong>Data Manipulation Language</strong>.
            DML commands are used to work with the actual records stored
            inside database tables.
        </p>

        <p>
            When we want to add a new record, change an existing record
            or remove a record, we use DML commands.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Common DML Commands
        </h5>

        <ul>

            <li class="mb-2">
                <strong>INSERT</strong> – Adds new records.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                <strong>UPDATE</strong> – Modifies existing records.
            </li>

            <li>
                <strong>DELETE</strong> – Removes records.
            </li>

        </ul>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            INSERT Example
        </h5>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>INSERT INTO Students
(
    StudentId,
    StudentName,
    Age
)
VALUES
(
    101,
    'Rahul',
    22
);</code></pre>

        </div>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            UPDATE Example
        </h5>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>UPDATE Students
SET Age = 23
WHERE StudentId = 101;</code></pre>

        </div>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            DELETE Example
        </h5>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>DELETE FROM Students
WHERE StudentId = 101;</code></pre>

        </div>


        <div class="alert alert-warning border-0 mt-4">

            <strong>Important:</strong>
            Always use a proper WHERE condition with UPDATE and DELETE
            when you intend to affect specific records.

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- DQL -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            3. DQL – Data Query Language
        </h2>

        <p>
            DQL stands for <strong>Data Query Language</strong>.
            It is used to retrieve data from database tables.
        </p>

        <p>
            The main command associated with data retrieval is
            <strong>SELECT</strong>.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            SELECT Example
        </h5>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students;</code></pre>

        </div>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Selecting Specific Columns
        </h5>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT StudentName, Age
FROM Students;</code></pre>

        </div>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Filtering Data
        </h5>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT *
FROM Students
WHERE Age &gt; 18;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- DCL -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            4. DCL – Data Control Language
        </h2>

        <p>
            DCL stands for <strong>Data Control Language</strong>.
            DCL commands are used to control access and permissions
            on database objects.
        </p>

        <p>
            Database administrators can use permissions to determine
            what operations a user or database principal is allowed
            to perform.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Common DCL Commands
        </h5>

        <ul>

            <li class="mb-2">
                <strong>GRANT</strong> – Gives permissions.
            </li>

            <li>
                <strong>REVOKE</strong> – Removes previously granted
                permissions.
            </li>

        </ul>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            GRANT Example
        </h5>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>GRANT SELECT
ON Students
TO UserName;</code></pre>

        </div>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            REVOKE Example
        </h5>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>REVOKE SELECT
ON Students
FROM UserName;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- TCL -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            5. TCL – Transaction Control Language
        </h2>

        <p>
            TCL stands for <strong>Transaction Control Language</strong>.
            It is used to manage transactions and control changes made
            during a transaction.
        </p>

        <p>
            A transaction is a logical unit of work containing one or
            more database operations.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Common TCL Commands
        </h5>

        <ul>

            <li class="mb-2">
                <strong>COMMIT</strong> – Permanently saves the changes
                made in a transaction.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                <strong>ROLLBACK</strong> – Undoes changes according to
                the transaction state.
            </li>

            <li>
                <strong>SAVE TRANSACTION</strong> – Creates a savepoint
                within a transaction.
            </li>

        </ul>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Transaction Example
        </h5>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>BEGIN TRANSACTION;

UPDATE Students
SET Age = 25
WHERE StudentId = 101;

COMMIT;</code></pre>

        </div>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            ROLLBACK Example
        </h5>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>BEGIN TRANSACTION;

UPDATE Students
SET Age = 30
WHERE StudentId = 101;

ROLLBACK;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- COMPLETE FLOW -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            SQL Command Categories Flow
        </h2>

        <p>
            The complete classification can be understood using the
            following diagram:
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>
                         SQL Commands
                              |
          ---------------------------------------------
          |             |             |        |      |
         DDL           DML           DQL      DCL    TCL
          |             |             |        |      |
      Structure        Data        Retrieve  Access  Transaction
          |             |             |        |      |
    CREATE          INSERT        SELECT    GRANT   COMMIT
    ALTER           UPDATE                  REVOKE  ROLLBACK
    DROP            DELETE
    TRUNCATE
            </code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- REAL WORLD UNDERSTANDING -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Real-World Understanding
        </h2>

        <p>
            Suppose we are developing a Student Management System.
            Different SQL categories will be used for different tasks.
        </p>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Requirement</th>
                        <th>Category</th>
                        <th>Command</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Create Students table</td>
                        <td>DDL</td>
                        <td>CREATE</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Add a new student</td>
                        <td>DML</td>
                        <td>INSERT</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Display students</td>
                        <td>DQL</td>
                        <td>SELECT</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Change student's age</td>
                        <td>DML</td>
                        <td>UPDATE</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Delete a student</td>
                        <td>DML</td>
                        <td>DELETE</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Give SELECT permission</td>
                        <td>DCL</td>
                        <td>GRANT</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>Save transaction changes</td>
                        <td>TCL</td>
                        <td>COMMIT</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- QUICK COMPARISON -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Quick Comparison
        </h2>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Category</th>
                        <th>Works With</th>
                        <th>Main Purpose</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>DDL</strong></td>
                        <td>Database Structure</td>
                        <td>Create / Change / Remove structure</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>DML</strong></td>
                        <td>Table Data</td>
                        <td>Insert / Update / Delete records</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>DQL</strong></td>
                        <td>Table Data</td>
                        <td>Retrieve records</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>DCL</strong></td>
                        <td>Permissions</td>
                        <td>Control access</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>TCL</strong></td>
                        <td>Transactions</td>
                        <td>Manage transaction changes</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- IMPORTANT POINTS -->
    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-info border-0">

            <h5 class="fw-bold">
                Important Points
            </h5>

            <ul class="mb-0">

                <li class="mb-2">
                    DDL mainly deals with database object structure.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    DML works with records stored in tables.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    SELECT is used to retrieve data.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    DCL is related to database permissions.
                </li>

                <li>
                    TCL is used to manage transactions.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- SUMMARY -->
    <section class="mb-4">

        <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Summary
            </h2>

            <p class="mb-0">
                In this section, we learned the major SQL command
                categories: DDL, DML, DQL, DCL and TCL. We understood
                the purpose of each category, studied their common
                commands and looked at practical examples of where each
                category is used in SQL Server.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


